1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:01,940
Your TV is off.

2
00:00:01,960 --> 00:00:03,260
The screen is black.

3
00:00:03,280 --> 00:00:04,600
The room is silent.

4
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:07,020
So why is the microphone still awake?

5
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:09,040
In September 2026,

6
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:11,600
a team of researchers left a brand-new LG

7
00:00:11,620 --> 00:00:13,580
TV sitting in standby,

8
00:00:13,860 --> 00:00:15,800
cut off its internet, and simply talked

9
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:16,940
around it.

10
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:19,240
Days later, they plugged the internet back in…

11
00:00:19,260 --> 00:00:21,980
and watched what the TV did next.

12
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,520
This isn't really a story about one brand.

13
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:26,420
It's about a machine we put in the most

14
00:00:26,440 --> 00:00:29,514
private room of our homes, and then forgot about.

15
00:00:29,534 --> 00:00:31,560
And stay till the end, because I'll give

16
00:00:31,580 --> 00:00:34,200
you five settings every smart TV owner

17
00:00:34,220 --> 00:00:35,780
should switch off tonight.

18
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:39,060
The last one is the one almost nobody does.

19
00:00:41,880 --> 00:00:44,960
On September 7th, 2026, the hardware

20
00:00:44,980 --> 00:00:47,520
channel Gamers Nexus published a video more

21
00:00:47,540 --> 00:00:49,100
than two hours long.

22
00:00:49,180 --> 00:00:51,600
They had teamed up with Level1Techs and a

23
00:00:51,620 --> 00:00:54,000
group of independent security researchers.

24
00:00:55,026 --> 00:00:56,300
Together, they spent more than five hundred

25
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:58,640
hours and roughly seventy thousand dollars

26
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:02,060
testing retail LG OLED televisions,

27
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:05,220
including the 2025 LG G5.

28
00:01:05,240 --> 00:01:08,000
They captured every packet the TVs sent,

29
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:09,480
decompiled the firmware,

30
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:12,040
and read the system logs line by line.

31
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:14,900
What they say they found falls into three parts.

32
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:17,500
The first is about what your TV hears.

33
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:19,340
The second is about what it sees inside

34
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:20,480
your house.

35
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:21,760
Not on the screen.

36
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:22,740
Inside your house.

37
00:01:22,760 --> 00:01:25,220
And the third is the scariest, because it

38
00:01:25,240 --> 00:01:27,200
isn't about LG at all.

39
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:28,880
It's about who else could get in.

40
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:30,520
Let's open the first box.

41
00:01:30,920 --> 00:01:33,360
First, how does a smart TV listen?

42
00:01:33,460 --> 00:01:35,940
Many LG TVs have a microphone in the

43
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:38,080
remote, and some have one built right into

44
00:01:38,100 --> 00:01:39,040
the TV.

45
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:41,700
That's called far-field voice recognition,

46
00:01:41,840 --> 00:01:44,220
the same idea as a smart speaker.

47
00:01:44,320 --> 00:01:46,880
It listens for a wake word, and for LG,

48
00:01:46,900 --> 00:01:48,340
that's "Hi LG."

49
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:49,820
Here's how it's supposed to work.

50
00:01:49,840 --> 00:01:52,760
A small, low-power chip keeps the last few

51
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:55,320
seconds of sound in a short memory buffer,

52
00:01:55,340 --> 00:01:56,960
like a loop of tape that keeps recording

53
00:01:56,980 --> 00:01:58,300
over itself.

54
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:00,540
It compares that loop to the wake word.

55
00:02:00,700 --> 00:02:01,560
No match?

56
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:03,360
The audio is thrown away.

57
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:04,180
Match?

58
00:02:04,400 --> 00:02:06,560
The TV wakes up and sends your command off

59
00:02:06,580 --> 00:02:07,660
to be understood.

60
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:09,900
Remember that loop of tape, because the

61
00:02:09,920 --> 00:02:11,840
whole story hangs on it.

62
00:02:11,860 --> 00:02:14,840
The question isn't whether your TV has a microphone.

63
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,920
It's what really happens to that loop when

64
00:02:16,940 --> 00:02:18,840
it's supposed to be thrown away.

65
00:02:18,883 --> 00:02:20,020
One more piece.

66
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:23,620
Many LG TVs have a feature called Always Ready.

67
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:26,760
When the TV looks off, it often isn't fully off.

68
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:29,860
It's in a low-power mode, ready to wake instantly.

69
00:02:29,940 --> 00:02:31,840
Which means that black screen in front of

70
00:02:31,860 --> 00:02:34,000
you might be the least honest thing in your

71
00:02:34,020 --> 00:02:35,020
living room.

72
00:02:35,200 --> 00:02:37,180
According to the investigation, with the

73
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:39,320
screen apparently off and the TV in

74
00:02:39,340 --> 00:02:40,140
standby,

75
00:02:40,360 --> 00:02:43,100
the set kept capturing audio from its microphone.

76
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:45,580
The researchers say it picked up background

77
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:48,400
sound from as far as twelve metres away.

78
00:02:48,680 --> 00:02:51,020
That's about forty feet, roughly the length

79
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:52,640
of an entire house.

80
00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:55,080
Then came the test I mentioned at the start.

81
00:02:55,220 --> 00:02:57,460
They cut the TV off from the internet.

82
00:02:57,500 --> 00:02:58,540
Think about it.

83
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:01,180
If a TV only listens for a wake word and

84
00:03:01,200 --> 00:03:03,280
deletes everything else, cutting the

85
00:03:03,300 --> 00:03:05,040
internet changes nothing.

86
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:06,980
There's nothing waiting to be sent.

87
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:09,060
But that's not what they say happened.

88
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:11,900
Audio and text files reportedly piled up in

89
00:03:11,920 --> 00:03:15,040
the TV's local storage while it was offline.

90
00:03:15,160 --> 00:03:17,380
And the moment the connection came back…

91
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:18,380
they uploaded.

92
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:20,280
And there was one clip in the report that

93
00:03:20,300 --> 00:03:22,040
people couldn't stop sharing.

94
00:03:22,220 --> 00:03:24,800
The researchers had finished talking to the TV.

95
00:03:25,020 --> 00:03:27,660
They'd moved on to a normal conversation.

96
00:03:27,920 --> 00:03:30,360
And the TV, they say, kept writing down

97
00:03:30,380 --> 00:03:33,580
what they were saying, word for word, as text.

98
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:36,240
Now, LG says this is not true.

99
00:03:36,260 --> 00:03:37,680
We'll get to their response.

100
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:39,720
And there's one word in it that most people

101
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:41,080
completely missed.

102
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:43,680
But first, the TV wasn't only listening to

103
00:03:43,700 --> 00:03:44,520
the room.

104
00:03:44,580 --> 00:03:46,780
It was also looking around your house.

105
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:49,500
Your TV sits on the same Wi-Fi as

106
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:51,220
everything else you own.

107
00:03:51,320 --> 00:03:53,340
According to the packet captures, the

108
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:55,920
tested TVs repeatedly scanned that network

109
00:03:55,940 --> 00:03:58,360
for other devices that have nothing to do

110
00:03:58,380 --> 00:03:59,900
with watching TV.

111
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:01,940
In the test lab, how many devices do you

112
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:02,980
think it found?

113
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:03,600
Ten?

114
00:04:03,370 --> 00:04:04,240
Twenty?

115
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:05,440
It found thirty-eight.

116
00:04:05,460 --> 00:04:06,320
Smartphones.

117
00:04:06,340 --> 00:04:07,240
Smartwatches.

118
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:08,540
A 3D printer.

119
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:09,820
An air purifier.

120
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:11,180
Even thermostats.

121
00:04:11,279 --> 00:04:13,480
The researchers say it also collected the

122
00:04:13,500 --> 00:04:16,200
names of nearby Wi-Fi networks, including

123
00:04:16,220 --> 00:04:18,440
your neighbours', along with their signal

124
00:04:18,460 --> 00:04:20,500
strength and location data.

125
00:04:20,520 --> 00:04:22,640
So why would a TV need to know the name of

126
00:04:22,660 --> 00:04:24,300
your neighbour's Wi-Fi?

127
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:26,540
Hold that thought, because the answer is

128
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:29,540
the reason this entire industry exists.

129
00:04:29,580 --> 00:04:31,600
According to the report, some of this data

130
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:33,580
went to LG Ad Solutions,

131
00:04:33,820 --> 00:04:36,540
which is LG's advertising business.

132
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:38,660
Now the third finding.

133
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:40,300
The researchers say they discovered

134
00:04:40,320 --> 00:04:42,740
remote-code-execution vulnerabilities in

135
00:04:42,760 --> 00:04:44,080
the TV's software.

136
00:04:44,560 --> 00:04:47,020
In simple words, these are bugs that could

137
00:04:47,040 --> 00:04:49,260
let an attacker run their own code on your

138
00:04:49,280 --> 00:04:51,540
TV without ever touching it.

139
00:04:51,560 --> 00:04:53,640
In a proof-of-concept demo, they showed

140
00:04:53,660 --> 00:04:56,120
that a compromised TV could switch on its

141
00:04:56,140 --> 00:04:58,840
built-in microphone while sitting in standby.

142
00:04:59,060 --> 00:05:01,460
So even if you trust LG completely,

143
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:03,880
the real question becomes this: do you

144
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:06,720
trust every hacker who finds the same bug?

145
00:05:06,740 --> 00:05:08,920
The researchers reported these flaws to LG

146
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:10,020
privately

147
00:05:10,120 --> 00:05:11,760
so they can be fixed first.

148
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:13,220
And if you think a government would never

149
00:05:13,240 --> 00:05:14,800
turn a TV into a spy…

150
00:05:14,820 --> 00:05:16,420
that already happened.

151
00:05:16,440 --> 00:05:17,420
Years ago.

152
00:05:17,440 --> 00:05:19,100
I'll show you in a moment.

153
00:05:19,540 --> 00:05:21,380
LG strongly denied the claims.

154
00:05:21,540 --> 00:05:23,800
It says its TVs do not continuously record

155
00:05:23,820 --> 00:05:26,360
or transmit conversations, and that voice

156
00:05:26,380 --> 00:05:27,980
is only processed when you hold the

157
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,400
remote's voice button, or say "Hi LG" after

158
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:33,140
turning that feature on.

159
00:05:33,260 --> 00:05:34,940
If no wake word is detected,

160
00:05:35,080 --> 00:05:38,560
LG says the audio is not stored or sent anywhere.

161
00:05:38,820 --> 00:05:41,280
LG also says content recognition,

162
00:05:41,420 --> 00:05:44,700
voice recognition and personalised ads are optional.

163
00:05:44,800 --> 00:05:46,160
Did you catch the word?

164
00:05:46,200 --> 00:05:46,920
Optional.

165
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:47,780
Not off.

166
00:05:48,260 --> 00:05:48,860
Optional.

167
00:05:48,860 --> 00:05:50,920
Critics pointed out that LG's statement

168
00:05:50,940 --> 00:05:53,340
didn't fully explain the network scanning,

169
00:05:53,360 --> 00:05:55,740
how much data is collected, or who it's

170
00:05:55,760 --> 00:05:56,700
shared with.

171
00:05:56,720 --> 00:05:57,320
So who's right?

172
00:05:57,320 --> 00:06:00,020
Honestly, until regulators independently

173
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:03,900
test these TVs, nobody can say for sure.

174
00:06:03,960 --> 00:06:06,180
But some of this isn't a claim at all.

175
00:06:06,200 --> 00:06:08,340
Some of it has already been proven.

176
00:06:08,660 --> 00:06:09,660
2015.

177
00:06:10,180 --> 00:06:12,660
Samsung's own privacy policy warned that if

178
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:14,500
your spoken words included sensitive

179
00:06:14,520 --> 00:06:15,560
information,

180
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:18,380
it could be captured and sent to a third party.

181
00:06:18,480 --> 00:06:19,520
2017.

182
00:06:19,920 --> 00:06:22,180
The US Federal Trade Commission said Vizio

183
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:24,500
had collected second-by-second viewing data

184
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:27,200
from around eleven million TVs without

185
00:06:27,220 --> 00:06:28,620
proper consent.

186
00:06:28,840 --> 00:06:31,720
Vizio paid two point two million dollars.

187
00:06:31,740 --> 00:06:33,220
Also 2017.

188
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:36,080
WikiLeaks published leaked CIA documents

189
00:06:36,100 --> 00:06:38,960
describing a tool called Weeping Angel.

190
00:06:39,100 --> 00:06:41,440
It could put certain Samsung TVs into a

191
00:06:41,460 --> 00:06:42,860
"Fake-Off" mode.

192
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,740
The screen went black, the lights went off…

193
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:47,720
and the microphone kept recording.

194
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:48,700
2019.

195
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,480
The FBI publicly warned that hackers could

196
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:54,360
take over smart TV cameras and microphones.

197
00:06:54,520 --> 00:06:56,320
And then there's the one your TV is

198
00:06:56,340 --> 00:06:59,140
probably doing right now: Automatic Content

199
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:01,380
Recognition, or ACR.

200
00:07:01,700 --> 00:07:04,680
Your TV can take pictures of what's on your screen.

201
00:07:04,780 --> 00:07:05,700
Not once.

202
00:07:05,720 --> 00:07:07,020
Over and over.

203
00:07:07,080 --> 00:07:10,100
A 2024 university study found Samsung TVs

204
00:07:10,120 --> 00:07:12,080
capturing the screen about twice every

205
00:07:12,100 --> 00:07:15,400
second, and LG TVs even more often.

206
00:07:15,420 --> 00:07:17,720
Even when the TV was just a monitor for a

207
00:07:17,740 --> 00:07:18,340
game console.

208
00:07:18,300 --> 00:07:20,200
In 2025,

209
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:24,500
Texas sued Samsung, LG, Sony, Hisense and

210
00:07:24,520 --> 00:07:26,220
TCL over it.

211
00:07:26,260 --> 00:07:27,460
Remember the question about your

212
00:07:27,480 --> 00:07:28,940
neighbour's Wi-Fi?

213
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:30,240
Here's the answer.

214
00:07:30,300 --> 00:07:32,340
Location and nearby devices help

215
00:07:32,360 --> 00:07:34,920
advertisers figure out who you are, where

216
00:07:34,940 --> 00:07:37,300
you live, and which phone belongs to the

217
00:07:37,320 --> 00:07:38,420
person watching.

218
00:07:38,740 --> 00:07:40,980
That lets an ad on your TV follow you to

219
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:41,820
your phone.

220
00:07:41,840 --> 00:07:43,400
And here's the part that will change how

221
00:07:43,420 --> 00:07:46,280
you look at that cheap 65-inch TV deal.

222
00:07:46,300 --> 00:07:48,080
In 2021,

223
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:50,300
Vizio revealed it was making more profit

224
00:07:50,320 --> 00:07:52,780
from ads and viewing data than from selling

225
00:07:52,800 --> 00:07:54,160
the TVs themselves.

226
00:07:54,660 --> 00:07:56,960
LG's ad business says it can reach more

227
00:07:56,980 --> 00:07:58,820
than three hundred and sixty million

228
00:07:58,840 --> 00:08:01,760
connected devices in the US alone.

229
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:03,880
So maybe the TV isn't the product.

230
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:05,200
Maybe you are.

231
00:08:05,460 --> 00:08:06,480
Here's the fix.

232
00:08:06,940 --> 00:08:09,160
Menu names change by model, but on an LG

233
00:08:09,180 --> 00:08:12,240
TV, open Settings, then General, and look

234
00:08:12,260 --> 00:08:13,160
for these.

235
00:08:13,354 --> 00:08:13,954
One.

236
00:08:13,680 --> 00:08:16,000
Turn off Always Ready and Quick Start, so

237
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:17,840
off actually means off.

238
00:08:18,020 --> 00:08:18,620
Two.

239
00:08:18,280 --> 00:08:19,820
Turn off voice recognition

240
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:22,960
and the "Hi LG" wake word if you don't use them.

241
00:08:22,980 --> 00:08:23,580
Three.

242
00:08:23,440 --> 00:08:24,820
Under User Agreements,

243
00:08:25,080 --> 00:08:26,700
untick viewing information, voice

244
00:08:26,720 --> 00:08:29,180
information, and personalised ads.

245
00:08:29,540 --> 00:08:30,980
On other brands, look for "viewing

246
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,680
information" or "content recognition."

247
00:08:34,020 --> 00:08:34,620
Four.

248
00:08:34,380 --> 00:08:37,000
Put your TV on a separate guest Wi-Fi, so

249
00:08:37,020 --> 00:08:39,200
it can't see your phones and laptops.

250
00:08:39,360 --> 00:08:41,840
And five, the one almost nobody does:

251
00:08:41,940 --> 00:08:43,980
don't connect your smart TV to the internet

252
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:44,940
at all.

253
00:08:45,020 --> 00:08:47,180
Plug in a streaming stick you trust, and

254
00:08:47,200 --> 00:08:49,260
let the TV just be a screen.

255
00:08:49,280 --> 00:08:51,500
But your TV isn't the only device in your

256
00:08:51,520 --> 00:08:53,820
home with an always-on microphone.

257
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:55,200
There's one in your pocket…

258
00:08:55,220 --> 00:08:58,220
and one you might soon be wearing on your face.

259
00:08:58,380 --> 00:09:00,300
That's what we're breaking down next.

260
00:09:00,720 --> 00:09:02,520
Subscribe so you don't miss it, and tell me

261
00:09:02,540 --> 00:09:05,820
in the comments: are you unplugging your TV tonight?
